module test.claim.hash_checkpoint_retired_witness

import v2.std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, SubstrateInputsOnly }

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = SubstrateInputsOnly

data hash_checkpoint_retired_witness_note: String = "PERMANENT REGRESSION CONTROL for the retired Hash checkpoint row (extdeps.languages.rust.types rust_checkpoint_hash_row_retired_note). While the row stood, a declaration spelled Hash realized as v1_rt::Hash, which is `pub type Hash = String` in the seed. The corpus declares exactly one such type, v2.std.node.Hash = Fnv1a64Structural, whose public record constructors are DELETED so that its `where lower_hex_16` refinement IS the construction wall -- so realizing it as a bare String alias rendered the carrier away and took a DESIGN 4b construction rung with it, silently. The live specimen was the emitted 05_eval closure: v2_std_node.rs carried `pub type Hash = v1_rt::Hash` while functions in the same file returned Rc<Fnv1a64Structural>, disagreeing at 70 distinct E0308 sites.

WHY THIS LIVES HERE RATHER THAN BESIDE THE EMITTER. The predecessor assertions lived in src/v1/tests/claim/checkpoint_identity_keying_witness_test.dag, and that tree DOES NOT EXECUTE: the required floor runs claim_executor with source roots dag and src/v2 only, src/v1 appears in no workflow, and a run log carrying 619 v2.test rows carries zero v1.tests rows. Seventeen witness files and 127 test fns sit there unexecuted. So the earlier repair -- which moved these assertions OUT of a generated #[cfg(test)] block that CI never compiles -- landed them somewhere CI never discovers, repeating the class it was written to correct one layer over. A control that does not execute is specification without execution, whatever its name says.

WHY BEHAVIOURAL RATHER THAN A DIRECT CALL. Asserting rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base returns none proves the lookup answers nothing; it does not prove the EMITTER stops writing v1_rt::Hash. This compiles a module and reads the emitted bytes, so it fails if any path -- checkpoint, alias renderer, or a future re-binding -- puts that spelling back. The bare spelling is the load-bearing input because the retired table was keyed on it, so the fixture declares its own Hash rather than importing v2.std.node: compile_dag_rust_emit_check compiles a virtual single-module source through the witness-root index, where importing v2.std.node refuses with hard diagnostics and would make this claim unrunnable rather than red. That is the same construction, and the same reason, as the sibling root_d_checkpoint_scalar_declared_arity_witness.

Dissolve-on: when lookup_checkpoint is re-keyed from a bare String to a resolved declaration identity, the spelling stops being the key and this fixture imports the authority instead."

fn w_hash_spelling_is_not_realized_as_the_seed_string_alias() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module hash_retired.probe\n\ntype Hash { digest: String }\n\nfn hash_retired_probe(h: Hash) -> Hash {\n  h\n}\n",
    "src/hash_retired_probe.rs",
    ["struct Hash"],
    ["v1_rt::Hash", "type Hash = String"]
  )
}

test fn hash_spelling_is_not_realized_as_the_seed_string_alias() -> Bool {
  w_hash_spelling_is_not_realized_as_the_seed_string_alias()
}
